{"name":"Old Map","hex":"#d5c9bc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mochi."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, concept, relationship, etc., having existed for a relatively long period of time.","Of a living being, having lived for most of the expected years."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old"},{"term":"map","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for Austronesian languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A visual representation of an area, whether real or imaginary, showing the relative positions of places and other features.","A graphical or logical representation of any structure or system, showing the positions of or relationships between its components."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/map"}],"entries":[{"title":"Map","blocks":[{"text":"In cartography, a map is a representation or abstraction of geographic reality. Maps are used for a wide range of purposes, including navigation, scientific research, governance, and warfare. A common use is route planning, which enables users to find their way to their destination. Specialized maps called charts are used for nautical navigation and aviation. Scientists use maps to organize spatial data in fields such as geology, meteorology, seismology and demographics. Governments use maps for administrative functions such as census, electoral districts, property taxation, dispatching police, and coordinating disaster relief. Military and security organizations use maps for mission planning, surveillance, border management, logistics, and intelligence analysis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/World_Map_1689.JPG/330px-World_Map_1689.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Map","wikidata":"Q4006"}],"images":[{"srcset":"ht